class Solution {
  public int priorityBasketEvenKeyCensus(int[] readings) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {PriorityQueue<Integer>heap=new PriorityQueue<>();for(int x:a)heap.add(x);while(!heap.isEmpty())out.add(heap.remove());}
    int answer=0;for(int x:out)if(x%2==0)answer++;return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build removalOrder

Put all numbers from `readings` into a min-heap. A min-heap is a tree-shaped collection that keeps its smallest number at the top. Remove the smallest number, record it, and repeat until the heap is empty. Keep repeated numbers. `removalOrder` is the removal order.

Return the answer

Count the even numbers in `removalOrder`. Store the count in `evenCount`. A number is even when it divides by 2 with no remainder. Complete `priorityBasketEvenKeyCensus` so it returns `evenCount`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `removalOrder` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n log n); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

